00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.049
In this video, you'll expand your NAT

00:00:02.049 --> 00:00:04.099
workflow to serve as a ReAct agent.

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:07.799
And you'll add Python tools for analyzing NOAA climate data.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.500
You'll register your functions, define input schemas with Pydantic,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.349
and you'll add descriptions so your ReAct

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:15.198
agent can determine which tools to use.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.800
You'll turn your agent from a simple chatbot

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.699
into one that takes intelligent data-driven actions.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.809
Alright, let's code.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.510
In this lesson we're going to build on what we learned previously.

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:28.210
This time we're going to build a climate analysis assistant.

00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:30.609
And we're going to use multiple tools with that assistant.

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:32.968
We're going to take plain Python functions

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:35.167
and we're going to turn them into NAT tools.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:37.114
And then we're going to register those

00:00:37.114 --> 00:00:38.768
tools to make them available to our agents.

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.868
We'll package all that up as a Python package,

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.254
and we'll also examine some other concepts

00:00:44.254 --> 00:00:46.067
such as how a ReAct agent uses tools.

00:00:47.097 --> 00:00:50.286
What is a ReAct agent? It's a common agentic pattern.

00:00:50.286 --> 00:00:52.296
It stands for reasoning and acting.

00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:54.597
And it's basically a loop.

00:00:54.896 --> 00:00:57.097
An LLM will reason about what to do next.

00:00:57.097 --> 00:01:00.296
It'll formulate one or more actions, it'll call those actions,

00:01:00.296 --> 00:01:01.947
and then it'll reason some more. And

00:01:01.947 --> 00:01:03.597
it'll continue that loop until it's finished.

00:01:03.832 --> 00:01:06.033
This ReAct Agent has access to three tools,

00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:07.819
and you'll notice that each of these

00:01:07.819 --> 00:01:09.632
tools is written in a completely different framework.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:12.733
When it runs, it'll decide which of these tools to use,

00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:16.033
and it'll iterate on tool use until it has a final answer.

00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:18.082
The data set we'll be working off

00:01:18.082 --> 00:01:19.632
of is real climate data from NOAA.

00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:22.233
And it includes information from multiple countries

00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:25.033
like the United States, France, Japan, Brazil.

00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:27.002
We'll introduce some plain Python functions

00:01:27.002 --> 00:01:28.832
that are able to analyze this data,

00:01:28.832 --> 00:01:31.933
and we'll see how to wire these functions into an agentic workflow.

00:01:32.516 --> 00:01:36.615
Python functions are the foundation of NAT tools.

00:01:37.115 --> 00:01:40.816
The top function is a vanilla Python function to calculate temperature statistics.

00:01:41.316 --> 00:01:44.316
It optionally takes a country and then it returns the statistics.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:48.490
If we want to use this as a NeMo Agent Toolkit tool,

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:50.531
we simply register it as a function,

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.850
and then we wrap it in some boilerplate,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.031
such as an async method and an async wrapper.

00:01:56.471 --> 00:01:59.150
Ultimately, it calls the calculate_statistics function,

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.271
and then we yield some FunctionInfo

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.430
to let the NeMo Agent Toolkit know how this function runs.

00:02:04.998 --> 00:02:07.498
It's a bit of boilerplate, but it's not too much.

00:02:07.697 --> 00:02:10.298
And now we're ready to wire it into our agentic workflow.

00:02:10.798 --> 00:02:12.897
The NeMo Agent Toolkit CLI can generate

00:02:12.897 --> 00:02:15.698
most of the boilerplate you need to wrap functions.

00:02:16.092 --> 00:02:17.997
But we're going to look at this here and

00:02:17.997 --> 00:02:19.901
then in a lot more detail on the slides.

00:02:20.602 --> 00:02:23.391
Every tool starts with a configuration class.

00:02:23.891 --> 00:02:26.591
This is the class that takes information in our

00:02:26.591 --> 00:02:29.292
YAML file and makes it available to the tool.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.830
Every tool also has an input schema.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.830
This is a Pydantic class that tells the large language model

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.631
or the agentic workflow how to call the tool.

00:02:39.531 --> 00:02:41.531
And finally, we have to decorate the function

00:02:41.531 --> 00:02:43.531
with a registration decorator.

00:02:43.741 --> 00:02:46.741
and this wires the tool into the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:02:47.241 --> 00:02:48.625
We will look at this in

00:02:48.625 --> 00:02:50.241
a lot more depth in the notebook.

00:02:50.941 --> 00:02:54.241
To package our workflows, we just use a standard Python project,

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.540
a pyproject.toml file.

00:02:56.941 --> 00:02:59.540
There's nothing NeMo Agent Toolkit specific here.

00:02:59.941 --> 00:03:02.941
We give the project a name, we specify entry points,

00:03:03.241 --> 00:03:06.241
and then we install it using pip or uv pip install.

00:03:06.522 --> 00:03:08.138
Once we have all of our

00:03:08.138 --> 00:03:10.022
tools registered in the NeMo Agent Toolkit,

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.323
we simply have to create a configuration file.

00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:14.107
In this configuration file, you can

00:03:14.107 --> 00:03:15.723
see we have a list of functions,

00:03:16.022 --> 00:03:18.022
and the top one is calculate_statistics.

00:03:18.022 --> 00:03:20.022
This is the tool we were just discussing

00:03:20.022 --> 00:03:22.622
that we wrapped to make it available to the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.000
There's a couple other tools as well.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.099
filter_by_country and create_visualization.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.699
These are similar functions that we've wrapped.

00:03:30.199 --> 00:03:33.000
You can see that our react_agent has gained some powers.

00:03:33.199 --> 00:03:37.000
It has a tool_names property, and we have passed the three functions

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.699
that we have defined above to the react_agent,

00:03:39.991 --> 00:03:42.790
now is ready to reason and act using these tools.

00:03:43.491 --> 00:03:46.491
Of course, the configuration file is not just about running the agent.

00:03:46.991 --> 00:03:49.991
While it provides tools, it also provides for telemetry,

00:03:50.491 --> 00:03:52.491
such as open telemetry tracing.

00:03:52.848 --> 00:03:54.753
and running evaluations against our agent to

00:03:54.753 --> 00:03:56.658
make sure it doesn't regress over time.

00:03:57.758 --> 00:04:01.359
The NeMo Agent Toolkit project is just a standard Python project.

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:03.778
You can use the NAT CLI to generate

00:04:03.778 --> 00:04:05.949
a lot of the code that we've looked at.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:10.079
For instance, this command, nat workflow create climate_assistant

00:04:10.462 --> 00:04:12.763
will create the following project structure.

00:04:13.062 --> 00:04:15.362
This is just a standard Python project

00:04:15.462 --> 00:04:17.463
that is ready to go with NAT.

00:04:17.663 --> 00:04:21.062
All right, let's add some intelligence to our agents with tools.

00:04:21.122 --> 00:04:24.002
First, let's look at some standalone climate tools.

00:04:24.622 --> 00:04:28.322
These are not agentic tools, these are just Python functions.

00:04:28.692 --> 00:04:31.822
We're going to import a series of Python functions here.

00:04:32.492 --> 00:04:34.293
that can load climate data,

00:04:34.692 --> 00:04:36.793
they can calculate climate statistics,

00:04:37.093 --> 00:04:39.093
they can filter climate data by country,

00:04:39.392 --> 00:04:41.793
find extreme years, create visualizations,

00:04:42.093 --> 00:04:44.793
and list the countries of the climate data.

00:04:45.492 --> 00:04:47.793
Let's take a look at what this climate data looks like.

00:04:48.192 --> 00:04:51.093
So we have 1210 temperature records

00:04:51.492 --> 00:04:54.093
ranging from 1950 to 2025

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:56.593
and spanning 10 countries.

00:04:57.144 --> 00:04:59.295
And now let's look at some of the functions.

00:04:59.795 --> 00:05:02.134
So the documentation for calculate_statistics says

00:05:02.134 --> 00:05:05.435
it calculates basic statistics from temperature data.

00:05:05.694 --> 00:05:08.115
It takes a data frame with temperature data

00:05:08.355 --> 00:05:10.935
and an optional country to filter it by

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:13.014
and it'll return a JSON string with the statistics.

00:05:13.845 --> 00:05:16.144
So let's call this function and see how it works.

00:05:16.952 --> 00:05:19.052
In this case we've called calculate_statistics

00:05:19.052 --> 00:05:21.963
and we've passed it the df, but not a country.

00:05:21.963 --> 00:05:24.163
Remember country is optional.

00:05:24.163 --> 00:05:25.963
And let's print the global statistics

00:05:25.963 --> 00:05:27.862
and we get some data output here.

00:05:28.221 --> 00:05:30.822
Now let's look at a different function, create_visualization.

00:05:31.221 --> 00:05:33.622
And this creates climate data visualizations

00:05:33.622 --> 00:05:36.122
and saves it to a file as an image.

00:05:36.422 --> 00:05:38.262
And we can see the arguments that

00:05:38.262 --> 00:05:40.103
it takes here and what it returns.

00:05:40.103 --> 00:05:42.702
Let's test this function out and see what it does.

00:05:43.103 --> 00:05:44.903
Here we see that calling create_visualization

00:05:45.302 --> 00:05:48.302
and we pass it a plot_type and a place to save

00:05:48.603 --> 00:05:51.903
will output a plot of climate visualization data.

00:05:52.202 --> 00:05:54.302
Each of these tools returns JSON.

00:05:54.603 --> 00:05:56.302
They have a single responsibility,

00:05:56.403 --> 00:05:58.802
but they are not plugged into our agentic workflow.

00:05:58.827 --> 00:06:00.927
Let's go ahead and do that.

00:06:01.028 --> 00:06:03.028
Now we're going to register a single tool

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:05.528
into our NeMo Agent Toolkit workflow.

00:06:05.827 --> 00:06:08.627
When you register a tool with the NeMo Agent Toolkit,

00:06:08.728 --> 00:06:10.927
there are three things that you need to consider.

00:06:11.194 --> 00:06:13.494
The first is the input to the tool.

00:06:14.194 --> 00:06:17.694
So NeMo Agent Toolkit will use pydantic to let you define

00:06:17.694 --> 00:06:20.694
what the input to the function looks like.

00:06:21.494 --> 00:06:23.994
So we're going to import BaseModel and Field from pydantic,

00:06:24.694 --> 00:06:26.994
and our tool is calculating statistics,

00:06:27.194 --> 00:06:31.293
so we will call this input definition CalculateStatsInput.

00:06:31.704 --> 00:06:36.204
and calculate stats takes a country that is optional.

00:06:36.603 --> 00:06:39.204
And so in our case, we will give it a description,

00:06:39.403 --> 00:06:42.803
country name to filter by, leave empty for global statistics.

00:06:43.283 --> 00:06:46.083
And now that we have our inputs defined, we can move on.

00:06:46.583 --> 00:06:48.382
Next, we'll set up our configuration.

00:06:49.283 --> 00:06:53.182
NeMo Agent Toolkit is config based and we've seen YAML files already.

00:06:53.283 --> 00:06:57.083
How do the properties from the YAML get into our tool

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:59.783
or our LLM or our workflow?

00:07:00.458 --> 00:07:02.028
it's through a config object.

00:07:02.728 --> 00:07:05.958
So in our case, we're going to define a CalculateStatisticsConfig.

00:07:06.418 --> 00:07:08.051
We'll give it a name, and

00:07:08.051 --> 00:07:09.958
this is going to become the type

00:07:10.437 --> 00:07:12.958
that we set in our YAML file.

00:07:13.531 --> 00:07:15.411
and we can give it a description.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:17.730
And in our case, we're just going to pass,

00:07:18.031 --> 00:07:19.790
which means there's no properties on this class,

00:07:20.031 --> 00:07:22.550
which means that our calculate statistics tool

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.331
does not take any configuration.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:26.032
But if it was going to

00:07:26.032 --> 00:07:27.831
take any configuration properties from the YAML,

00:07:27.831 --> 00:07:29.662
we would define them here. And now

00:07:29.662 --> 00:07:31.494
we're going to register the tool itself.

00:07:32.194 --> 00:07:35.494
We register with a decorator, register_function,

00:07:36.194 --> 00:07:38.894
and we pass into that decorator the config_type,

00:07:39.093 --> 00:07:41.293
which is that config that we created earlier.

00:07:41.697 --> 00:07:45.976
This is a bit of boilerplate and the NAT CLI

00:07:46.497 --> 00:07:49.576
has generate commands that will generate some of it for you.

00:07:49.937 --> 00:07:51.312
We're not going to talk in

00:07:51.312 --> 00:07:52.916
detail about all the aspects of this,

00:07:53.156 --> 00:07:55.576
but we'll see here that we have a wrapper function

00:07:55.776 --> 00:07:57.152
that wraps the Python function that

00:07:57.152 --> 00:07:58.757
we're going to expose as a tool.

00:07:58.976 --> 00:08:00.509
It takes two properties that we'll

00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:02.297
talk about in greater depth later on.

00:08:02.504 --> 00:08:04.904
the configuration which will be injected in.

00:08:05.305 --> 00:08:07.151
And if we had properties on

00:08:07.151 --> 00:08:09.305
our configuration object that we defined earlier,

00:08:09.605 --> 00:08:11.855
then this would be an argument that

00:08:11.855 --> 00:08:14.105
contains those properties pulled from the YAML.

00:08:14.404 --> 00:08:16.704
And then a builder which we can use to grab

00:08:16.704 --> 00:08:19.305
other aspects of our workflow such as an LLM

00:08:19.504 --> 00:08:21.166
or a retriever or any other

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:23.105
thing that we've defined in our YAML.

00:08:23.404 --> 00:08:25.704
In our case, we're not going to be using either of these.

00:08:26.004 --> 00:08:29.004
We are going to load our climate data as a data frame.

00:08:29.213 --> 00:08:31.114
And then as we wrap our tool,

00:08:31.114 --> 00:08:33.014
we will pass that data frame in.

00:08:33.714 --> 00:08:35.560
Here's the actual function that we're

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.714
wrapping, calculate_statistics that we looked at earlier.

00:08:38.413 --> 00:08:39.706
We pass in the data frame

00:08:39.706 --> 00:08:41.214
that we loaded a few lines above.

00:08:41.714 --> 00:08:43.307
And then you see that

00:08:43.307 --> 00:08:46.807
we are also accepting a country parameter.

00:08:47.607 --> 00:08:50.807
This is optional of course, as we defined earlier,

00:08:51.207 --> 00:08:54.908
and we will pass that country in as well to calculate statistics

00:08:54.908 --> 00:08:59.240
and then we'll return the result. So we've wrapped the Python function,

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.340
but we still need to describe it to the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:09:02.639 --> 00:09:05.039
And so what we will do from this is

00:09:05.039 --> 00:09:07.340
we will yield a FunctionInfo object.

00:09:07.908 --> 00:09:11.528
and the function that we'll pass to that FunctionInfo object

00:09:11.528 --> 00:09:13.128
is the _wrapper that we just looked at.

00:09:13.618 --> 00:09:15.918
And the input_schema that we talked about earlier

00:09:16.388 --> 00:09:18.687
that defines what the input looks like to our tool,

00:09:18.955 --> 00:09:22.754
and a description, and this description will help the agentic workflow

00:09:22.754 --> 00:09:24.654
know whether it should call this tool or not.

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:26.805
So a bit of boilerplate code, but

00:09:26.805 --> 00:09:28.754
again, this would be generated for you.

00:09:28.754 --> 00:09:30.201
A lot of it would be generated

00:09:30.201 --> 00:09:31.855
for you by the NeMo Agent Toolkit CLI.

00:09:32.686 --> 00:09:36.385
If you wanted to see and run the full register.py file,

00:09:36.385 --> 00:09:38.280
you could run this cell here and we would

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.385
be able to see what the Python file looks like.

00:09:40.985 --> 00:09:43.086
Now let's look at what the configuration looks like.

00:09:43.186 --> 00:09:45.485
We have this configuration already saved to disk,

00:09:45.485 --> 00:09:46.547
so we're going to load it

00:09:46.547 --> 00:09:47.785
into our cell and take a look.

00:09:48.385 --> 00:09:50.086
And here's the config file that we're

00:09:50.086 --> 00:09:51.785
going to use for this NAT workflow.

00:09:52.056 --> 00:09:54.316
Some of it will already be familiar to you.

00:09:55.015 --> 00:09:58.115
the climate_llm that we looked at in the previous notebook.

00:09:58.515 --> 00:10:00.716
But we have a new section and this is called functions.

00:10:01.015 --> 00:10:02.188
And these are the tools that

00:10:02.188 --> 00:10:03.556
we can make available to our agents.

00:10:04.024 --> 00:10:07.124
and the first and only function we have listed here

00:10:07.224 --> 00:10:09.224
is calculate_statistics.

00:10:09.524 --> 00:10:10.908
And we give it a type

00:10:10.908 --> 00:10:12.524
and we looked at this type earlier

00:10:12.823 --> 00:10:15.823
simple_tool_demo/calculate_statistics.

00:10:15.923 --> 00:10:18.124
This is how the NeMo Agent Toolkit

00:10:18.224 --> 00:10:20.524
knows how to find this tool in our Python functions.

00:10:20.927 --> 00:10:23.726
and we can override the description here for the tool.

00:10:24.126 --> 00:10:26.927
Our workflow is similar but different than before.

00:10:27.427 --> 00:10:30.726
So you'll recall before the type was just a simple LLM call,

00:10:30.826 --> 00:10:34.226
and now we're telling it that the workflow is a react_agent.

00:10:34.802 --> 00:10:37.701
A ReAct agent reasons and then acts in a loop,

00:10:37.802 --> 00:10:41.001
and this is a built-in agent to the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:10:41.402 --> 00:10:43.601
So we're going to say run a ReAct agent

00:10:43.701 --> 00:10:47.201
and the ReAct agent is going to have the following tools available.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:51.551
and we pull the tool from the functions listed above.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.750
So we have calculate_statistics here

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.951
and we have passed that in as a tool to our react_agent.

00:10:57.051 --> 00:11:01.150
It also takes an LLM, which we defined earlier, the climate_llm.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:03.800
And we're going to set verbose to true, and this way

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.350
we'll be able to see detailed outputs for the tool use.

00:11:06.551 --> 00:11:08.212
All right, let's go ahead and

00:11:08.212 --> 00:11:10.150
install and run our ReAct Agent workflow.

00:11:10.515 --> 00:11:12.039
So the tools that we looked

00:11:12.039 --> 00:11:13.816
at earlier do need to be installed

00:11:13.816 --> 00:11:16.015
into our virtual environment.

