just so we'll be ready to run other packages and other notebooks.

00:12:21.327 --> 00:12:23.347
So let's talk through what we did.

00:12:23.427 --> 00:12:25.947
To register a Python function as a tool,

00:12:26.327 --> 00:12:29.028
we need a configuration class with a unique name.

00:12:29.528 --> 00:12:31.327
We need an async wrapper function.

00:12:31.912 --> 00:12:35.611
we need to yield a FunctionInfo with an LLM description

00:12:35.711 --> 00:12:38.412
and then we can reference it in our config file

00:12:38.511 --> 00:12:40.412
as package_name/tool_name.

00:12:41.111 --> 00:12:43.111
You can use NAT workflow create

00:12:43.211 --> 00:12:46.097
to create all the scaffolding you've done by hand in this notebook.

00:12:46.097 --> 00:12:48.498
And if you want more information on that,

00:12:48.498 --> 00:12:51.898
you can run NAT workflow create dash dash help.

00:12:52.298 --> 00:12:54.462
Here's a configuration file that has all of

00:12:54.462 --> 00:12:56.898
the tools that we looked at earlier registered in.

00:12:57.264 --> 00:13:01.063
So we have calculate_statistics, which we've previously seen,

00:13:01.464 --> 00:13:04.063
but now we've wired in the other tools as well.

00:13:04.264 --> 00:13:06.264
list_countries, filter_by_country,

00:13:06.464 --> 00:13:09.063
find_extreme_years, create_visualization.

00:13:09.456 --> 00:13:13.056
These are just sub properties of the functions top level property.

00:13:13.456 --> 00:13:15.956
And then we'll pass them into our ReAct agent here.

00:13:16.255 --> 00:13:19.956
Previously we just had calculate_statistics listed as a tool.

00:13:20.355 --> 00:13:22.405
But now we've listed all of the tools

00:13:22.405 --> 00:13:24.456
and made them available to our ReAct agent.

00:13:24.703 --> 00:13:26.803
We've also made two other small changes.

00:13:27.203 --> 00:13:29.053
Since we have many tools, sometimes the

00:13:29.053 --> 00:13:30.903
ReAct agent needs to iterate several times.

00:13:31.303 --> 00:13:33.604
We've told it that it can iterate up to five times.

00:13:34.203 --> 00:13:36.354
And then also, sometimes it will call

00:13:36.354 --> 00:13:38.504
a tool incorrectly and have to retry.

00:13:38.803 --> 00:13:41.504
and we've told it that it can retry up to two times.

00:13:42.153 --> 00:13:44.653
All right, now that we have all of our tools registered,

00:13:44.653 --> 00:13:47.952
we are ready to run more complicated queries against our agent.

00:13:48.552 --> 00:13:50.753
First, let's ask about global warming trends.

00:13:51.153 --> 00:13:52.752
We're going to run NAT run and

00:13:52.752 --> 00:13:54.352
we'll pass it that long configuration file

00:13:54.352 --> 00:13:56.302
we just looked at with the input of

00:13:56.302 --> 00:13:58.253
what is the global temperature trend per decade.

00:13:58.913 --> 00:14:01.064
And we can see the ReAct Agent reasoning

00:14:01.064 --> 00:14:03.214
down through the tools that we provided it,

00:14:03.683 --> 00:14:05.313
decides to call calculate_statistics

00:14:05.813 --> 00:14:08.974
and it gets us the global temperature trend per decade.

00:14:09.573 --> 00:14:11.274
Now let's ask for country analysis.

00:14:11.711 --> 00:14:14.510
In this case, we're going to ask about a specific country,

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:17.211
specifically France's climate data.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.311
Let's see if it'll use multiple tools during this run.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.811
We can see it filtering by country.

00:14:23.073 --> 00:14:25.474
and we get the results back from the tool

00:14:25.673 --> 00:14:27.673
of specific country data.

00:14:28.573 --> 00:14:30.774
And then we see it calculating statistics,

00:14:30.874 --> 00:14:33.173
and this time it's passing a specific country,

00:14:33.274 --> 00:14:35.974
which is France, and it gets data back from that.

00:14:36.403 --> 00:14:38.191
And now we see the LLM take the

00:14:38.191 --> 00:14:40.202
provided JSON that it got from the various tools

00:14:40.202 --> 00:14:41.772
and put it together into a

00:14:41.772 --> 00:14:43.602
final answer, which it returns to us.

00:14:43.903 --> 00:14:46.202
Let's test the visualization tools next.

00:14:46.403 --> 00:14:48.202
In this case, we're going to ask for a visualization

00:14:48.202 --> 00:14:50.903
showing which countries have the highest warming trends.

00:14:51.602 --> 00:14:54.903
And we can see that it's chosen to call create_visualization.

00:14:55.311 --> 00:14:56.603
And it's let us know that

00:14:56.603 --> 00:14:58.110
it has saved that to a file

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:00.811
and that file is ready for us to view.

00:15:01.311 --> 00:15:04.110
Let's take a look at what it rendered.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:06.510
And here we can see it gave us a rendering

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:08.811
of the top five countries by warming trend.

00:15:09.432 --> 00:15:12.331
Finally, let's give it a really difficult question

00:15:12.631 --> 00:15:14.293
that's definitely going to require multiple

00:15:14.293 --> 00:15:16.231
tools and see how it handles it.

00:15:16.932 --> 00:15:18.425
In this case, we want it to

00:15:18.425 --> 00:15:20.131
compare the temperature trends of Canada and Brazil

00:15:20.142 --> 00:15:22.831
to answer the question which one is warming faster

00:15:22.841 --> 00:15:25.631
and then also create a visualization of global trends.

00:15:26.073 --> 00:15:27.915
And we can see it reasoning

00:15:27.915 --> 00:15:30.063
down through calculating the statistics per country,

00:15:30.364 --> 00:15:32.264
first for Canada, then for Brazil.

00:15:32.663 --> 00:15:34.864
We can see it having to retry a few times.

00:15:35.563 --> 00:15:38.163
But we finally get to the point where it's able to save

00:15:38.354 --> 00:15:41.254
a PNG image of the global trends

00:15:42.153 --> 00:15:43.953
and give us a final response

00:15:44.254 --> 00:15:48.051
giving us the trend per decade. And let's look at that output.

00:15:48.051 --> 00:15:51.551
And here we can see a plot of temperature over year.

00:15:52.551 --> 00:15:54.201
And now we'll do some cleanup. So

00:15:54.201 --> 00:15:55.850
let's delete the images that we've created.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.250
And let's uninstall the workflow that we created

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:02.250
just so it doesn't conflict with later notebooks in the course.

00:16:03.051 --> 00:16:05.051
In this notebook, we've seen how we can take

00:16:05.051 --> 00:16:06.850
standalone Python functions

00:16:07.051 --> 00:16:09.051
and wrap them as tools to be used by

00:16:09.051 --> 00:16:11.551
NeMo Agent Toolkit agents.
